","arithmetic-transport/modes.html":"<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>PerfectPower · Interference microscope</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="/arithmetic/style.css"><style>.phase-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:18px}.hist-bars{display:flex;align-items:end;gap:12px;height:180px;border-bottom:1px solid #547581}.hist-bars div{flex:1;background:#78e5b9;min-width:2px;position:relative}.hist-bars span{position:absolute;bottom:-28px;left:0;font-size:12px}.phase-zero{color:#ff9d9d}.mode-cards{display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap}.mode-cards span{background:#234859;border-radius:8px;padding:7px 12px;font-size:13px}.phase-label{font-size:20px;color:#ffdfa4}.critical{display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin:18px 0}.critical button{background:#234859;color:white}.critical button.active{background:#79e5bb;color:#102936}@media(max-width:650px){.phase-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}}</style></head><body><main><nav><a href="/arithmetic/">← Two-mode path explorer</a><a href="/research">All research</a></nav><p class="eyebrow">EXACT INTERFERENCE MICROSCOPE</p><h1>More paths.<br>Less signal?</h1><p class="intro">Count every allowed route, then combine its ordered Pauli contributions. Opposite phases cancel even when both routes reach the same number.</p><section class="controls"><label>Experiment<select id="experiment"></select></label><label>Exact height ceiling<select id="ceiling"></select></label></section><div id="modes" class="mode-cards"></div><section class="metrics" style="margin-top:22px"><article><span>Complete allowed population</span><strong id="mode-count"></strong></article><article><span>Net coherent contribution</span><strong id="net"></strong></article><article><span>Compiled arithmetic states</span><strong id="states"></strong></article></section><section class="scene"><p id="message" class="phase-label"></p><div class="phase-grid"><div><h2>Opposite phases</h2><div id="bars" class="hist-bars"></div></div><div><h2>Every ceiling, one sweep</h2><svg id="phase-plot" viewBox="0 0 500 220" aria-label="Exact population and net contribution across height thresholds"></svg><p>Green: route population. Gold: magnitude of the net Pauli coefficient. Each curve is scaled to its own maximum.</p></div></div><div class="critical" id="critical"></div></section><section class="scene"><h2>A route from the exact population</h2><svg id="route" viewBox="0 0 700 240" aria-label="Arithmetic height along a selected route"></svg><div class="transport"><button id="play-mode">Play route</button><button id="sample-mode">Uniformly sample another</button><span id="mode-state"></span></div><p id="path-phase"></p></section><section class="account"><h2>The new mathematical result</h2><p>With two copies of each move, the tight-ceiling population has <b>three routes and a nonzero signed coefficient when q ≥ p²</b>. When p &lt; q &lt; p², it has <b>two routes whose contributions cancel exactly</b>. That general threshold theorem is now proved in Lean.</p><p>The model supports several prime modes, rational heights and restrictions on move order. These are exact finite word-channel calculations. They do not assert measured quantum behavior of integers or a Riemann-hypothesis result.</p><p id="turns"></p><a href="https://github.com/towre676-cloud/perfectpower/blob/main/docs/ARITHMETIC_MODES_MONOGRAPH.md">Read the expanded monograph →</a><button id="download-mode">Download the complete exact experiment</button><p id="mode-sync"></p></section></main><script src="/arithmetic/engine.js"></script><script src="/arithmetic/modes-engine.js"></script><script src="/arithmetic/modes-app.js"></script></body></html>
